# Root Caddy config for the VPS, at /opt/caddy/Caddyfile. Reference copy only:
# the deploy workflow never ships this file — it changes rarely, and it is
# shared ground between every app on the host, so it is applied by hand
# (deploy/README.md has the runbook).
#
# Each app's vhost lives in its own sites/*.caddy file, owned and shipped by
# that app's repo. This repo owns sites/mtg.caddy and nothing else;
# sites/social.caddy belongs to the social app. The split is what lets either
# app deploy without being able to clobber the other's routing.
{
	servers {
		# Load-bearing for the app's rate limiter, which identifies a client by
		# the address in X-Forwarded-For. Caddy already discards a client's own
		# copy of that header by default and appends only the peer address it
		# observed; this narrows trust further, to requests arriving from
		# private ranges. An internet client's address is public, so it can
		# never be a trusted proxy.
		#
		# Widening this to 0.0.0.0/0 would let anyone on the internet choose
		# their own rate-limit identity — evading their own budget and spending
		# somebody else's. backend/tests/test_room_lookup_limits.py reads this
		# file and fails if it happens.
		trusted_proxies static private_ranges
	}
}

import sites/*.caddy
